ion: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\n2: 1, A: 1, K: 1, 10: 2, 8: 2, 6: 1\nValid Moves:\npair: ♥3 ♦3\npair: ♥4 ♣4\npair: ♣7 ♦7\nsingle: ♦3\nsingle: ♥4\nsingle: ♥5\nsingle: ♣7\nsingle: ♥9\nsingle: ♥2\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: This is a free play (no previous combination to beat), so I can play any valid move. My hand is weak, consisting mostly of low cards and only one high card (♥2). As a peasant, my goal is to help my teammate (who has 6 cards) win by either getting rid of my cards efficiently or setting up opportunities for them to play safely. The landlord has only 2 cards left, so they are very close to winning — this means urgency is critical.\n\nPromising options:\n- Playing a pair like ♥3♦3 or ♥4♣4 could help clear my hand faster and are safe, low plays that likely won’t steal control from my teammate.\n- Playing a single low card like ♥5 or ♥9 might allow my teammate (who may have more useful cards) to take control with a higher card.\n- However, since it\'s a free play, playing a pair is generally better than a single, as it clears two cards at once and reduces hand size more efficiently.\n- Playing the ♥2 now would be disastrous — it’s the highest single card besides the jokers, and if the landlord doesn\'t have a higher card, they might be forced to use a bomb or rocket unnecessarily. But I should save such power unless necessary.\n\nMy teammate just passed after playing a pair (♠7♥7), suggesting they may be trying to shed mid-range pairs or may not have strong singles. They still have 6 cards, so coordination is key.\n\nBest move: Play a low pair to shed two cards without wasting high power.\n\nReason: Playing the pair ♥3♦3 is optimal because it removes two cards from my hand efficiently, keeps higher cards (like ♥2) in reserve for later control, and avoids overcommitting power. It also signals to my teammate that I’m clearing small cards, possibly inviting them to continue building on sequences or controlling with stronger cards.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥3", "♦3"]}'}
